from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "Performance degradation occurs because the `record_once` mechanism in Flask\'s blueprint system only prevents re-registration when the same blueprint instance is registered multiple times, not when different blueprint instances register the same template filter.\\n\\nWhen a blueprint registers a template filter using `add_app_template_filter()` or the `@app_template_filter()` decorator (found in `src/flask/sansio/blueprints.py` lines 461-475), it uses `record_once()` which wraps the registration function. The `record_once` method (lines 233-244) ensures the registration callback only executes when `state.first_registration` is `True`. However, `first_registration` is determined by checking if the blueprint instance (`self`) has been registered before (line 316: `first_bp_registration = not any(bp is self for bp in app.blueprints.values())`).\\n\\nIf multiple distinct blueprint objects are created (even with the same name), each is a different Python object. When each blueprint instance is registered, `first_bp_registration` will be `True` for each one because the identity check (`bp is self`) fails for different instances. This causes each blueprint\'s filter registration callback to execute, repeatedly assigning to the same dictionary key: `state.app.jinja_env.filters[name or f.__name__] = f` (line 473).\\n\\nDuring application startup, this results in:\\n1. **Unnecessary dictionary operations**: Each blueprint instance overwrites the same filter entry in `app.jinja_env.filters`, causing redundant dictionary assignments\\n2. **Wasted initialization time**: The deferred function execution and dictionary updates occur multiple times when they only need to happen once\\n3. **Potential memory churn**: While Python dictionaries handle overwrites efficiently, repeated assignments during startup add unnecessary overhead\\n\\nThe impact on `render_template` execution time during concurrent requests is minimal because:\\n- Filter registration happens during application initialization, not during request handling\\n- Once registered, filter lookup during template rendering is a simple O(1) dictionary lookup from `jinja_env.filters`\\n- Flask uses thread-local storage for request contexts, so concurrent requests don\'t interfere with each other\'s template rendering\\n\\nHowever, the performance degradation primarily affects application startup time, which can be significant if many blueprint instances are created and registered, each attempting to register the same filters. This is particularly problematic in test scenarios where multiple blueprint instances might be created for different test cases.\\n\\nThe test case `test_blueprint_url_defaults` (lines 131-148 in `tests/test_blueprints.py`) demonstrates this scenario where the same blueprint instance is registered twice with different names, but the issue would be more pronounced if different blueprint instances were created for each registration."}' + '\n')
